KNOWNBUG: N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/3 + [dcl.init]/17 + [temp.variadic]/4-5.  A
variadic constructor taking a forwarding-reference parameter pack `_U&&... __e`
that forwards each element into a recursive base's constructor (exactly
libstdc++ std::tuple's constructor over `_Tuple_impl<_Idx,_Head,_Tail...> :
_Tuple_impl<_Idx+1,_Tail...>`) stores the WRONG element values when the deduced
pack has DISTINCT element types (`tuple<int,double>`): get<0> reads a
nondeterministic value instead of the constructor argument, so both `==11` and
`!=11` can fail.  A same-typed pack (`tuple<int,int>`) and a by-VALUE variadic
constructor pack (cpp17_tuple_get_two_pack_ctor_3elem, CORE) are stored
correctly, so the defect is specific to a forwarding-reference (`_U&&`)
parameter pack forwarded through recursive base construction with heterogeneous
types.  This is the root of cpp17_tuple_basic (std::make_tuple(1,2.0,'a') then
std::get<0> reads the wrong value; two-element tuples use a non-variadic
specialization, so 3+ elements are the first to hit it).  g++/clang++ store 1.
Flip to CORE once such a forwarding-reference variadic constructor pack forwards
heterogeneous elements into a recursive base correctly.
